---
/**
 * THE THEME DECISION: one theme, light, and no toggle.
 *
 * Why: this design system's dark mode is explicitly not designed yet.
 * globals.css declares `@custom-variant dark (&:is(.dark *))` but ships no
 * `.dark` token block at all, so there is nothing for a dark theme to render
 * as. Keeping Starlight's toggle would have offered a switch into its stock
 * blue-grey dark palette, which shares nothing with the landing page — and the
 * landing page has no toggle, so the two halves of one site would disagree
 * about whether the feature exists. A missing control looks deliberate; a
 * control that leads somewhere unstyled looks broken.
 *
 * Why this file rather than CSS alone: Starlight's Page.astro hardcodes
 * `data-theme="dark"` on the <html> element and is not in the overridable
 * component list. The colour mapping in src/styles/site.css is unlayered, so
 * the palette is already correct whatever that attribute says — but Starlight
 * gates a few things on the attribute itself rather than on the variables,
 * most visibly which Expressive Code theme paints the code blocks. Setting it
 * here settles those too.
 *
 * This replaces Starlight's own ThemeProvider, which is where the localStorage
 * and `prefers-color-scheme` lookup lived. The <template id="theme-icons"> it
 * also rendered is only read by ThemeSelect, which is likewise overridden away
 * — so nothing is left looking for it.
 *
 * Known limit: with JavaScript disabled the attribute stays at Page.astro's
 * `dark`. The page still renders in the design's palette, because the mapping
 * is CSS; only the code blocks fall back to Starlight's dark syntax theme.
 */
---

{/* Inlined, and in <head>, so the attribute is set before the first paint. */}
<script is:inline>
  document.documentElement.dataset.theme = "light"
</script>
